.......KIT-142 methods paragraph (draft, from the record):
6 data sets from 4 omics were combined: lipidomics (lipidomics_LCMS_Pos, lipidomics_LCMS_Neg; normalised, not corrected for unwanted variation); metabolomics (metabolomics_LCMS_Pos, metabolomics_GCMS; normalised, not corrected for unwanted variation); proteomics (proteomics; normalised, not corrected for unwanted variation); transcriptomics (transcriptomics; normalised, not corrected for unwanted variation). Shared factors were learned with multi-omics factor analysis, MOFA2 1.22.0 (mofapy2 0.7.3, Python 3.12.16, R 4.6.1), in double precision. Samples were matched across omics by the biological-sample column each omic saved, corrected by a user-supplied match table that assigned 40 runs to another biological sample (SHA-256 1b56aaae41af71f121e60bc8ae4fdd5a7237672b0edebfad1ab120fc93daea2c). Runs of the same biological sample were averaged (the mean of the observed values). Internal standards were removed (0 features). Biological samples missing from a data set were kept, with that data set's values missing. Features measured in fewer than 10 of the 20 biological samples (a share of 0.5) were left out. In each data set with more than 1000 features, the 1000 whose variance lies furthest above the data set's trend of variance with mean abundance (a lowess fit of each feature's variance on its mean, span 0.3) were used, a feature selection that also makes the data sets more equal in size (a smaller data set kept all its features), each scaled to mean 0 and standard deviation 1 after the selection (which used the unscaled values); in simulation, with few samples and missing values this scaling improved the recovery of weak factors, and otherwise made no practical difference. The model was asked for 5 factors (convergence mode slow, at most 10000 training rounds, seed 20261119; each data set divided by its overall standard deviation: yes; features centred: yes). Variance explained refers to the selected features of each data set, not to every feature measured. Training settled after 731 rounds, stopped by its convergence rule, and kept 5 factors; 20 biological samples entered the model, 19 of them measured in every data set. Training ran with the maths library on one thread and one fixed code path (OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1, OMP_NUM_THREADS=1, MKL_NUM_THREADS=1, OPENBLAS_CORETYPE=Haswell), under which models were bit-identical across the processor models tested (three AMD models; an Intel processor not yet tested); the same data, settings and seed give the identical model on the same software. The model was trained 5 times, from seeds 20261119 to 20261123 (the declared seed and the next whole numbers), and the model reported is that of seed 20261119. Each factor's agreement across seeds is the absolute correlation of its values with the most similar factor of each other model (factors paired one to one, the most similar pair first); the lowest agreement per factor was Factor1 1.000, Factor2 1.000, Factor3 1.000, Factor4 1.000, Factor5 1.000. Each factor was related to the experimental groups after training: a one-way analysis of variance of the factor's values on the group, with the share of the factor's variance that lies between the groups (R-squared) as the effect size, and p-values adjusted across all the factors of the model (Benjamini-Hochberg). Within each factor and data set, features were ranked by the absolute value of their weight and the 10 highest were listed; since the sign of a factor is arbitrary, a feature's direction has meaning only against the other features of the same factor. These lists are exploratory: their order was not tested for stability. Scatter plots show these features' values as given to the model (the runs of a biological sample averaged, after feature selection and any per-feature scaling, before MOFA2 centres and scales each data set; lipidomics_LCMS_Pos: scaled per feature; lipidomics_LCMS_Neg: scaled per feature; metabolomics_LCMS_Pos: scaled per feature; metabolomics_GCMS: scaled per feature; proteomics: scaled per feature; transcriptomics: scaled per feature) against the factor's values, with missing values left out; the features were chosen from the same model. Heatmaps show the same values with each feature centred on its mean, for display only, the samples in order of the factor's values. The factors were learned without the groups; their association with the groups describes the model. The data sets entered the model as their normalised values before correction for unwanted variation (RUV), so unwanted variation that is correlated with the groups was not removed and can contribute to a factor's association with the groups. Settings that were provisional defaults at the time of the analysis: Runs of the same biological sample (averaged); Internal standards (removed); Samples missing from a data set (kept, with that data set's values missing); Centre each feature on its mean (yes); Number of factors (5); Random seed (20261119); Number of models trained (one per seed) (5).
